  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-10-29.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="024621"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#024621" label="[02:46:21]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ooh interesting</span></div><div class="line" id="025414"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025414" label="[02:54:14]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmmmm</span></div><div class="line" id="032133"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#032133" label="[03:21:33]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm still very conflicted on my thoughts about the hardware bootstrapping problem</span></div><div class="line" id="032258"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#032258" label="[03:22:58]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i don't take the view including bootstrapping the whole fabrication process - my position is to treat the fabrication process as a black box that introduces some kind of distrust (and then on-die firmware like intel ME as a lot more distrust)</span></div><div class="line" id="032341"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#032341" label="[03:23:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&amp; so depending on the level of distrust you're comfortable with you have different starting points</span></div><div class="line" id="032429"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#032429" label="[03:24:29]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i can't decide whether something like a fpga-emulated riscv core is sufficient (which also introduces the problem of compiling the core)</span></div><div class="line" id="032452"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#032452" label="[03:24:52]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or whether actual hand-built hardware is at all worthwhile</span></div><div class="line" id="032713"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#032713" label="[03:27:13]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think in terms of trust I'd rather use cross-compiling so it's a bit like Diverse Double Compiling, hardware edition. Although there should still be the goal of finding a trustworthy computer.</span></div><div class="line" id="033013"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033013" label="[03:30:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I tend to agree in terms of mitigating distrust of hardware</span></div><div class="line" id="033057"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033057" label="[03:30:57]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">only really feasible for open architectures I think though</span></div><div class="line" id="033113"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033113" label="[03:31:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or otherwise many-impls</span></div><div class="line" id="033307"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033307" label="[03:33:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(some problem with DCC now, clang/gcc duopoly in the (open) compiler land makes it difficult to effectively execute DCC)</span></div><div class="line" id="033316"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#033316" label="[03:33:16]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And part of the problem is even if say you'd have a trustworthy computer but only capable of disassembling programs to check the work of less trustworthy computers, you'd then need a lot of human time for inspection.</span></div><div class="line" id="033453"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033453" label="[03:34:53]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">right</span></div><div class="line" id="033526"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033526" label="[03:35:26]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">these are difficult problems, that's for sure :D</span></div><br /></div></body></html>